---
/**
 * Global Scientific Canvas — the page-level background system (v1.7).
 *
 * v1.6 replaced a faint ambient layer with a visible one, which fixed "you
 * cannot see it" but created the next problem: eight independent plates, each
 * with its own title, sitting on the page like figures pinned to a wall. The
 * page read as a research document, not as somebody's work.
 *
 * v1.7 keeps the visibility and throws away the plates. Three compositions,
 * read as three movements of one drawing:
 *
 *   HeroComposition    biology → dynamics → AI, connected by a single line
 *   MidComposition     a measured object becoming a computational one
 *   LowerComposition   the drawing thinning out as the page ends
 *
 * Each is one SVG, cropped by its own frame, bleeding off at least one page
 * edge, with no captions. Continuity comes from the drawings entering and
 * leaving their frames rather than from a single stretched viewBox, which would
 * distort at every viewport.
 *
 * Placement contract:
 *
 *   body (position: relative)
 *     └── <GlobalScientificCanvas />   ← this file, z-index: -1
 *     ├── <Header />
 *     ├── <main>…</main>
 *     └── <Footer />
 *
 * `position: absolute`, not `fixed`: the drawing stays attached to the parts of
 * the page it belongs to and scrolls with them. A fixed layer reads as
 * wallpaper.
 *
 * Accessibility and performance are unchanged from v1.6: aria-hidden,
 * pointer-events: none, inline SVG only — no canvas, no WebGL, no image, no
 * network request — and every stroke resolves to a theme token.
 */
import HeroComposition from './HeroComposition.astro';
import MidComposition from './MidComposition.astro';
import LowerComposition from './LowerComposition.astro';
---

<div class="global-science" data-global-scientific-canvas aria-hidden="true">
  <HeroComposition />
  <MidComposition />
  <LowerComposition />
</div>
